Agent: Per your saved preferences, I have forwarded all of your tax details and questions to **vince2000@gmail.com**. 

While Vince will be able to give you the most comprehensive advice, here is a general breakdown of how your situation is typically handled for the 2023 tax year:

**1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing**
For 2023, the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is **$27,700**. Your listed itemized deductions (like the $1,400 charitable donation to St. Mary's Food Bank) fall well below this amount. Therefore, it will likely be much more beneficial for you to take the **Standard Deduction**. 

**2. What You Can Deduct (Above-the-Line & Business Deductions)**
Even if you take the standard deduction, you can still take certain "above-the-line" deductions and business deductions:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You can deduct up to **$2,500** of your $3,600 student loan interest as an adjustment to income, provided your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) doesn't exceed the phase-out limits for joint filers.
*   **Unreimbursed Expenses ($890):** If these are related to your W-2 job at Riverside Hospital, they are unfortunately no longer deductible on your federal return due to the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. However, if they are related to your IT contracting work, they can be fully deducted as business expenses.

**3. Treating the Home Office**
Because you have self-employment income (the $15,000 from IT contracting), you can claim a home office deduction against that specific income. You have two choices:
*   **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot for up to 300 sq ft. For your 140 sq ft, this would be a flat **$700** deduction.
*   **Regular Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business (140 sq ft / 750 sq ft = **18.6%**). You can deduct 18.6% of your rent ($1,720/mo), utilities, and renter's insurance. This method requires more record-keeping but will likely yield a higher deduction for you.

**4. Forms You Will Likely Need**
*   **Form 1040:** The main US individual income tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** For reporting your additional income (contracting) and adjustments to income (student loan interest).
*   **Schedule B:** Because your interest income ($1,560 + $320 = $1,880) exceeds $1,500, you must report it here.
*   **Schedule C:** To report your $15,000 IT contracting income and deduct your business expenses (unreimbursed expenses and home office).
*   **Form 8829:** Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you choose the Regular Method for your home office).
*   **Schedule SE:** To calculate self-employment tax on your IT contracting net income.

Vince should be reaching out to you shortly with the next steps! Let me know if you need me to send him any additional details.
